mt7986-qdma-autorate
====================

This repository contains two OpenWrt packages:

  kmod/qdma-rl             kmod-qdma-rl, kernel module           GPL-2.0-only
  controller/qdma-autorate qdma-autorate, controller + service   GPL-2.0-only
                           (its BPF object, from ePPing:         GPL-2.0-or-later)

The full GPL-2.0 text is in LICENSE.


ePPing (BPF passive ping) - third-party code
--------------------------------------------

controller/qdma-autorate/src/pping_kern.c, pping.h, pping_debug_cleanup.h and
include/xdp/parsing_helpers.h are vendored from

  xdp-project/bpf-examples, https://github.com/xdp-project/bpf-examples
  commit 63a943e088b704c8c21649992931df795f4b9e5a
  (pping/ and include/xdp/parsing_helpers.h)

Their upstream license and copyright headers are kept unchanged
(GPL-2.0-or-later; parsing_helpers.h: GPL-2.0-or-later OR BSD-2-Clause).

Changes made to the vendored code, so it runs on a stock OpenWrt kernel
(no CONFIG_PERF_EVENTS, no kernel BTF) and gives enough samples for downloads:

  1. Ring buffer instead of perf buffer: the events map is
     BPF_MAP_TYPE_RINGBUF and the program uses bpf_ringbuf_output()
     (stock OpenWrt kernels have no CONFIG_PERF_EVENTS).
  2. No CO-RE: with -DPPING_NO_CORE the preserve_access_index attribute is
     dropped, so the object has no CO-RE relocations (no
     /sys/kernel/btf/vmlinux on stock OpenWrt). The map-cleanup iterator
     programs, which need kernel BTF, are not loaded; the controller removes
     stale timestamps from user space instead.
  3. Compiler flag: built with -mllvm -bpf-disable-trap-unreachable, because
     clang 21 otherwise emits a weak __bpf_trap typed-ksym extern, which makes
     libbpf require kernel BTF.
  4. Static subprograms: under PPING_NO_CORE, parse_packet_identifer_tc/_xdp
     are static, because without kernel BTF the verifier rejects global
     functions that take a ctx pointer.
  5. Pure-ACK timestamping: a ts_pure_acks config flag (on in this
     controller) also timestamps pure ACKs. A download client sends almost
     nothing else, and stock ePPing only timestamps packets with payload.

The controller (autorate.c, ctl.c, ctl.h) is new code that loads this BPF
object; it does not use ePPing's user-space program.


MediaTek QDMA scheduler register fields
---------------------------------------

Register offsets, the per-queue max-rate fields, the queue scheduler-select
bits (MTK_QTX_SCH_TX_SEL_V2) and the rate encoding come from the Linux
6.12.112 mtk_eth_soc driver with the OpenWrt 25.12.6 patches.

The field layout of the hardware scheduler rate registers (tx_sch_rate),
which upstream does not define, is taken from MediaTek's OpenWrt feed:

  mediatek/mtk-openwrt-feeds, commit c2b24b480
  25.12/files/target/linux/mediatek/patches-6.12/
    999-eth-90-mtk_eth_soc-support-proprietary-debugfs.patch
      MTK_QDMA_TX_SCH_RATE_EN / _RATE_MAN / _RATE_EXP (one 16-bit half per
      scheduler) and the scheduler-id -> word/half mapping
    999-eth-20-mtk_eth_soc-add-HQoS-configuration-restore.patch
      save/restore of qtx_sch and both tx_sch_rate words

Only these definitions are used, for register writes from kmod-qdma-rl. No
driver code is changed and no kernel rebuild is needed.
